    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122">

                <pb facs="0196" n="184" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg184"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/1806: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cudahy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-03-12T18:00:00+01:00">March 12, 1940—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 12—2:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. From an unimpeachable source I am advised that Germany has decided that no
                    acceptable peace terms can be expected from France and Great Britain and is
                    therefore determined to bring off large-scale military offensive. My informant
                    states he has no information of approximate date but Belgian Ambassador<anchor
                        n="48a" xml:id="d122-f1"/> in Berlin states order has gone forth to prepare
                    German hospitals for reception of large number of casualties to be expected.</p>
                <p>Copy to the Legation at The Hague.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cudahy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d122-f1" n="48a" xml:id="d122-f1-content">
                <p>Vicomte Jacques Davignon.</p>
